\chapter*{Abstract}
\addcontentsline{toc}{chapter}{Abstract}
This work closes part of the formal gap between the Lorentzian projector-refinement framework and its proposed cosmological dark sector.  At finite refinement depth, level-orthogonal symmetry and branch homogeneity select the order-$2\sigma$ fiber operator up to one coefficient.  Average-field parity then selects the leading analytic interaction in that spectral channel, while an independent order-zero coupling remains allowed.  The resulting generally covariant action has an exact stress-exchange identity.  Its matter Hamiltonian bracket closes, the complete minimally coupled constraints obey the hypersurface-deformation algebra, and an alias-free resolved $H$--$H$ realization closes to numerical precision under declared spectral headroom.  The rapid-crossing continuum interpolation is generalized to arbitrary refinement order.  A Mellin analysis shows that a fixed-prime discrete tower retains log-periodic amplitude corrections rather than converging pointwise to that interpolation.  The frozen Volume II benchmark is then stress-tested.  Its shape-preserving energy projection has normalization $\BridgeEta$, so the unprojected spectrum exceeds the available crossing energy by $\BridgeOverdraw$.  A direct discrete order-one backreaction threshold moves the system into a sparse, colder regime and does not preserve the benchmark spectrum.  One-loop power counting also requires a finite physical cutoff or ultraviolet cancellation.  These results strengthen the covariant effective theory while narrowing the physical claim.  They do not establish a microscopic value of the coupling, a self-consistent production history, or an empirical dark-sector identification.

